      en: "Kaminaljuyú II is an archaeological site in the Guatemalan highlands, representing one of the largest and longest-occupied Maya settlements. Inhabited from around 800 BCE to 950 CE, it was a major political and economic center, controlling trade routes for obsidian and jade. The site features over 200 mounds, including a 18-meter pyramid and a ball court. Excavations have revealed complex water management systems and tombs with rich offerings, indicating a hierarchical society. Kaminaljuyú II was influenced by Teotihuacan in the Classic period, evident in architectural styles and artifacts.",
